> kit at that version; it matches this project as long as `chassis_tag` in
> `.chassis.toml` reads `{{ chassis_tag }}`.

{{ name }} is built on [chassis]({{ chassis_repo }}), a shared foundation
for small Rust web services. Everything below comes from the kit and works
the same way in every service built on it. What {{ name }} does itself —
its routes, its pages, its own configuration — is described in the
project's own README and `docs/`.

## The door: how callers get in

Every request to a protected route carries a **client token**:

```bash
curl -sS -H 'Authorization: Bearer <client token>' \
     -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
     -d '{}' http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/…
```

A client token is issued per caller, on the dashboard's **Clients** page
(a name, one click) or, for a service without a dashboard, with
`chassis clients` on the machine that runs it. The token is shown once and
can be revealed, copied, re-issued or revoked at any time; revoking locks
the caller out the same second. A request without a valid token is
answered with HTTP 401 and a JSON body that says what to send.

Two secrets belong to the operator, not to callers, and live only in the
environment file (`{{ env_file }}`), never in a flag or a config file:

- `{{ prefix }}_TOKEN` — the **admin token**: it logs you in on `/login`
  and also works as a bearer for scripts run by the operator.
- `{{ prefix }}_SECRET_KEY` — the **secret key** (32 bytes, hex) that seals
  the stores on disk: clients, sessions, passkeys.

Both come from one command, run on a terminal (it refuses a pipe, so a
secret never lands in a log):

```bash
{{ name }} gen-secret
```

Paste the two lines it prints into `{{ env_file }}`. Without both, a
service with a dashboard refuses to start and `--check` tells you so.

## The dashboard (`dashboard` feature)

A server-rendered set of pages behind the admin login:

| Page | What it is for |
|---|---|
| `/login` | Enter the admin token; a session cookie follows. |
| `/` | The status page: service, health, updates, problems, then the project's own sections. |
| `/clients` | Issue, reveal, copy, re-issue, revoke and delete client tokens; see each client's last requests. |
| project pages | Whatever {{ name }} adds under its own navigation entries. |
| `/passkeys` | Register a passkey and log in with it (`passkeys` feature; only over HTTPS through a trusted proxy). |

The layout, the navigation and the **theme picker** (kp-themes
{{ kp_themes }}) come from the kit; a chosen theme is remembered by the
browser.

## Health and metrics

`GET /healthz` is open and answers JSON with the running version and one
entry per subsystem. It is HTTP 200 while everything is fine and **503**
as soon as one subsystem reports a failure (`"status":"degraded"`):

```bash
curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz
# {"status":"ok","version":"0.1.0","subsystems":{"store":{"detail":"writable","ok":true}}}
```

`GET /metrics` is open and serves Prometheus text: request counts per
route and status, build info, uptime, and whatever {{ name }} appends.

`{{ name }} --healthcheck` probes `/healthz` of the running instance from
the command line (the container `HEALTHCHECK` and the systemd unit use it)
and exits 0 when a well-formed report came back — degraded still counts
as alive; dead does not.

## Self-update (`self-update` feature)

The service can fetch its own next release, verify it and swap the binary
in place. `{{ prefix }}_UPDATE_MODE` picks who pulls the trigger:

- `off` (default): the status page shows whether a newer release exists;
  nothing is installed.
- `supervised`: the homelab runs `{{ name }} update` on its schedule. That
  one run verifies, installs and exits **0 whether or not anything
  changed** (`already current` is a success); it never restarts the
  service — the supervisor does, after `installed …`.
- `autonomous`: the running process checks on a timer, installs, exits 0
  and lets `Restart=always` start the new binary; a version that does not
  come up healthy is rolled back to the previous binary.

Every release is verified against the **signing key baked into the
binary** before a single byte of it is trusted: the manifest's signature
first, then the binary's SHA-256 against that manifest. A release that
does not verify is refused with nothing installed.

`{{ prefix }}_UPDATE_HOLD` pins a version (`1.4.0`) or skips one
(`skip:1.4.0`); `{{ prefix }}_UPDATE_DRILL` is for the broken-release
drill only. Before an update the kit keeps a copy of the state under
`{{ state_dir }}-pre-update/<version>/`.

## Notifications (`notify` feature)

The kit emits events and posts each to the webhooks that subscribe to it:
`service.started` after the bind, `update.installed`, `update.ok`,
`update.failed`, `update.rolled_back`, `update.held`, and
`health.degraded` / `health.recovered` once per transition. {{ name }} may
add events of its own.

Webhooks are declared in the config file, one table per target:

```toml
[[notify.webhook]]
events = ["update.*", "health.degraded"]
url = "https://hooks.example.lan/{{ name }}"
headers = { "Authorization" = "Bearer ${{ '{' }}{{ prefix }}_HOOK_TOKEN}" }
```

`${VAR}` in a URL or header is read from the environment when the service
starts, so a secret stays out of the file. Delivery retries with backoff
and never blocks a request.

## Command-line controls

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `{{ name }} --version` | Prints `{{ name }} <version>`; reads nothing else. |
| `{{ name }} --check` | Validates the configuration and the project's own checks; opens no socket; exit 0 or 1. |
| `{{ name }} --print-config` | Every knob with its effective value and where it came from (flag, env, file, default); secrets masked. |
| `{{ name }} --knobs` | The table below, for the version of the kit this binary was built with. |
| `{{ name }} --healthcheck [URL]` | Probes `/healthz` of the running instance; exit 0 when alive. |
| `{{ name }} gen-secret` | Prints a fresh `{{ prefix }}_TOKEN` and `{{ prefix }}_SECRET_KEY` (terminal only). |
| `{{ name }} update` | One supervised update attempt; exit 0 when current or installed. |
| `{{ name }} --help` | Every flag, including one per knob. |

## Configuration knobs

Every knob answers to three names: a flag, an environment variable and a
key in the config file (`{{ state_dir }}/config.toml`). Precedence is
**flag > env > file > default**. Secret knobs have no flag and are refused
from the file. The **Feature** column says which kit feature reads the
knob; a knob of a feature this binary was built without is accepted and
ignored.

{{ knobs_table | trim }}

## Where things live

- **State**: `{{ prefix }}_STATE_DIR`, default `{{ state_dir }}`. The
  config file, the sealed stores and the update state all live under this
  one directory; nothing is written anywhere else.
- **Environment file**: `{{ env_file }}`, read by the systemd unit
  (`EnvironmentFile=`). It holds the two secrets and any knob you set as
  an environment variable.
- **Pre-update copies**: `{{ state_dir }}-pre-update/`, beside the state
  root, not under it.

